WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.069
-Our first guest tonight is an
Emmy and Peabody Award-winning

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:07.039
journalist who hosts "Anderson
Cooper 360" weeknights on CNN.

00:00:07.039 --> 00:00:08.908
His "New York Times"
best-selling book, "Vanderbilt:

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:11.677
The Rise and Fall of
an American Dynasty" is out now.

00:00:11.677 --> 00:00:13.880
Please welcome back to the show
our friend Anderson Cooper.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:17.183
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:26.826
♪♪

00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:28.194
Welcome back.

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:30.162
-How are you doing?
-I'm doing really well.

00:00:30.162 --> 00:00:34.067
I feel like this is --
[ Cheers and applause continue ]

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:36.035
Very happy you're here.
-Thank you.

00:00:36.035 --> 00:00:37.636
-This is maybe
a byproduct of the fact

00:00:37.636 --> 00:00:39.138
that I see you on TV
all the time.

00:00:39.138 --> 00:00:41.040
I did not realize you have
not been here since 2017.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.275
-I had no idea.
-That's a long break.

00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:43.810
-I see you on TV all the time.
-There you go.

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:45.612
-It's better that way, really.
-It's better that way, yeah.

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:47.714
[ Laughter ]
This is already awkward, yeah.

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:49.348
[ Laughter ]
But congratulations.

00:00:49.348 --> 00:00:50.950
You have had a baby boy
since I last saw you.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.251
-I have, yes.
-Here's Wyatt right there.

00:00:52.251 --> 00:00:54.954
Look at this guy.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:54.954 --> 00:00:57.890
18 months.
-He's 18 months old.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.759
-Pretty good time?
-It's the best thing ever.

00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:00.794
-Talking, chatting?

00:01:00.794 --> 00:01:02.661
-Yeah, lots of, you know...

00:01:02.661 --> 00:01:05.898
"Shoes" is his favorite word
and "paintings."

00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:07.132
He says, "Au revoir."

00:01:07.132 --> 00:01:09.235
-Oh, he says "au revoir?"
-The latest thing he --

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:13.806
Just yesterday we taught him,
"Where did it go?"

00:01:13.806 --> 00:01:17.076
And so he just goes around
going, "Where did it go?"

00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:18.244
[ Laughter ]

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:20.046
He doesn't know what it means,
but...

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:21.948
-He knows it's like a button.

00:01:21.948 --> 00:01:22.882
He's like it's the perfect thing
to say.

00:01:22.882 --> 00:01:24.049
-And it goes up.
-Yeah.

00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:25.284
-Yeah.
-Halloween.

00:01:25.284 --> 00:01:27.186
What are you guys going to do?

00:01:27.186 --> 00:01:28.354
-Halloween seems to be like --

00:01:28.354 --> 00:01:30.222
It's like an arms race
with these kids

00:01:30.222 --> 00:01:32.158
and the Halloween costumes,
especially with, like,

00:01:32.158 --> 00:01:34.027
well-known people's children.

00:01:34.027 --> 00:01:37.363
They seem to put pictures of
them out on --

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:39.632
It's like a -- Yeah.
-And if you haven't planned

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:40.733
in advance, it's already
too late.

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:42.335
-Oh, my God.
-Supply chains are down.

00:01:42.335 --> 00:01:43.769
-Yes, I know.
-It is too late.

00:01:43.769 --> 00:01:46.238
-So, a friend of mine named
Chip Kidd, who actually did

00:01:46.238 --> 00:01:48.775
the book cover of "Vanderbilt"
and is an old friend

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:51.678
of my mom's,
he is a Batman-obsessed

00:01:51.678 --> 00:01:53.412
and has an amazing
Batman collection.

00:01:53.412 --> 00:01:58.584
He gave me -- or he gave Wyatt
a costume his mom made for him

00:01:58.584 --> 00:02:00.887
when he was a kid probably
in the early '70s.

00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:02.354
-Wow.
-It's a homemade cape --

00:02:02.354 --> 00:02:04.523
Batman cape with,
like, a cheap plastic --

00:02:04.523 --> 00:02:06.292
Do you remember those --
I don't know how old you are,

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:08.194
but those Halloween costumes
when I was a kid were just,

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:11.998
like, a weird one-dimensional
mask and a tiny piece of elastic

00:02:11.998 --> 00:02:13.299
that just go around it.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:15.501
-It was back when people thought
a really healthy thing

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:16.970
to breathe all night
was plastic.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:18.037
-Yes, exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:19.838
It was completely flammable.

00:02:19.838 --> 00:02:21.540
So that's what I bought --
I have for Wyatt.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.476
-There you go.
-A very flammable costume.

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:26.879
-I feel like vintage fashion
is, like, the highest end,

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:29.215
whereas vintage Halloween
is, like, very low end.

00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:30.716
[ Laughter ]

00:02:30.716 --> 00:02:32.151
-I'm trying to bring that back.

00:02:32.151 --> 00:02:34.353
-Yeah, low end, cheap Halloween.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:34.353 --> 00:02:36.655
I tried it on him
and he wasn't really thrilled

00:02:36.655 --> 00:02:37.957
with the whole mask yet.

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:39.525
-Yeah, he'll get used to it
if he wants his candy.

00:02:39.525 --> 00:02:41.628
-I think it's the kind of thing
he'll look back on and think,

00:02:41.628 --> 00:02:44.630
"Oh, that's cool. I was wearing
a vintage Batman costume."

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:46.766
-I love that you are now
officially a parent

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:49.769
because your logic is, "You will
look back on this and like it."

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:51.337
[ Laughter ]

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:53.840
"It's not my fault you can't
appreciate the present."

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.675
[ Laughter ]

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:57.644
What about -- I have heard this.

00:02:57.644 --> 00:02:59.945
We're kind of the same,
I'll admit.

00:02:59.945 --> 00:03:01.180
Try to limit screen time.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.149
-Yes, I'm obsessed with
limiting screen time.

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:05.585
Although it's hard because,
you know, if I'm at work,

00:03:05.585 --> 00:03:08.488
occasionally, in between
commercial breaks, whatever,

00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:10.489
I'll just call to check on
how he's doing.

00:03:10.489 --> 00:03:14.393
And he's not allowed to
look at a screen --

00:03:14.393 --> 00:03:16.229
Not allowed.
I mean, we try to avoid it.

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:19.565
But he will now grab the screen
while I am FaceTiming with

00:03:19.565 --> 00:03:23.302
Benjamin, my former partner,
and he will run with it

00:03:23.302 --> 00:03:26.338
like a crazy albino monkey.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:26.338 --> 00:03:28.874
And it's the greatest thing
ever.

00:03:28.874 --> 00:03:30.410
Like, I love it because it --

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:32.311
It's like his point of view
of the world.

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:33.579
-Yeah.
-And he's just like...

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:37.583
[ Babbling ]
[ Laughter ]

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:39.318
And it's -- Yeah, I love it.

00:03:39.318 --> 00:03:40.619
-I haven't been able to --

00:03:40.619 --> 00:03:42.855
I'm trying to impress this
upon my boys,

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:46.492
which is when they FaceTime
with my parents, they do that

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:48.494
and I want to -- like, I can
tell my parents just need to

00:03:48.494 --> 00:03:49.628
get off the phone because
they're, like, old

00:03:49.628 --> 00:03:50.829
and it's giving them a headache.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:52.531
For them, that's
a roller coaster of like,

00:03:52.531 --> 00:03:53.766
"Let me show you
what I've been doing."

00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:55.468
And they're like,
"Well, you know what?

00:03:55.468 --> 00:03:59.505
Send us a still photo."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:02.675
You, as a young man,
you dressed up a lot.

00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:04.843
I mean, I guess
this is very in line with

00:04:04.843 --> 00:04:07.413
a Vanderbilt upbringing.
-Yeah, it is weird.

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:09.815
I look back and all the photo --
I sent some because

00:04:09.815 --> 00:04:12.451
all the photos of my childhood,
I'm wearing costumes,

00:04:12.451 --> 00:04:14.420
and this isn't for Halloween.
-This is not Halloween.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:16.222
This is just around the house.

00:04:16.222 --> 00:04:17.323
-This was any given day.
-That's normal.

00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:18.991
That's normal.
[ Audience aws ]

00:04:18.991 --> 00:04:20.726
-Yeah, that was
my knight outfit.

00:04:20.726 --> 00:04:22.795
-You're doing a very good job
here of not smiling.

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:25.130
[ Laughter ]
-That was my theater outfit.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:28.735
My brother wearing
my knight outfit also.

00:04:28.735 --> 00:04:29.935
Yeah.
-So there you go.

00:04:29.935 --> 00:04:31.137
So that was always part of it.
-Yeah, it was.

00:04:31.137 --> 00:04:33.706
It was the '70s.
This was the early '70s, and --

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:35.975
-Kids dressed up like knights
in the '70s.

00:04:35.975 --> 00:04:37.577
[ Laughter ]
-Yes.

00:04:37.577 --> 00:04:39.077
-What was trick-or-treating --
Growing up in New York City,

00:04:39.077 --> 00:04:40.313
what was trick-or-treating
in New York like in the --

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:42.114
-People think it's weird.
I would --

00:04:42.114 --> 00:04:44.016
My friend Michael Youckto lived

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:46.185
across the street
in a high-rise.

00:04:46.185 --> 00:04:49.689
And so we would -- I would go
over there and trick-or-treat.

00:04:49.689 --> 00:04:52.057
And it was great
because the elevator --

00:04:52.057 --> 00:04:53.993
That was back when -- like,
buildings then had

00:04:53.993 --> 00:04:57.262
elevator operators,
and they would put up a list

00:04:57.262 --> 00:04:59.132
in the elevator of
who wanted to be bothered

00:04:59.132 --> 00:05:00.599
and who didn't want
to be bothered.

00:05:00.599 --> 00:05:04.003
So you knew your success rate
was like 100%.

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:05.838
Because you wouldn't bother
with, like, the old lady

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:08.474
who didn't want to, you know,
have you come to the door.

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:10.075
-Was it about 50/50?

00:05:10.075 --> 00:05:12.011
Like, what percentage of
New Yorkers in the '70s

00:05:12.011 --> 00:05:14.680
were cool with kids in costumes
coming to their door?

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:17.183
-You know, in the building
I went in, it was fine.

00:05:17.183 --> 00:05:19.084
-Alright.
-But, yeah, you know.

00:05:19.084 --> 00:05:20.720
-Were you nervous?
I mean, I feel like everybody --

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:23.989
-Like, 20% of the time you
got blades in the apples.

00:05:23.989 --> 00:05:25.591
[ Laughter ]
But I mean.

00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:26.692
-That's fine.
-But it was totally --

00:05:26.692 --> 00:05:27.894
No one thought about it then.

00:05:27.894 --> 00:05:30.463
Like, it was just --
it was unwrapped candies.

00:05:30.463 --> 00:05:33.399
-Yeah. Just like --
-Marathon bars.

00:05:33.399 --> 00:05:35.234
Remember those long
Marathon bars?

00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:37.202
-Oh, you got a Marathon bar,
that's a day maker.

00:05:37.202 --> 00:05:40.239
I have a lot to ask you about,
especially the book.

00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:41.908
We'll be right back with
more from Anderson Cooper.

